<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100654 (level 12). Domain: threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="threat_hunting,linux,mitre_gaps,">

  <!-- R5 T1562.001 - stopping/disabling a security or logging service, purging
       auditd rules, disabling SELinux/AppArmor.

       REVISION 2026-07-26 - the former regex required the verb BEFORE the
       service name:
         (stop|disable|mask)\b.{0,60}(auditd|wazuh|...)
       But both SysV syntaxes put the verb last:
         service auditd stop        -> a0="service"    a1="auditd" a2="stop"
         rc-service auditd stop     -> same
       Those commands therefore did NOT match: the bypass was simply to use
       `service` instead of `systemctl`. The regex is now bidirectional (verb
       before OR after the service name).

       SECOND DEFECT, measured live: the constraint was on `audit.exe`, with
       `/service$` in the list. But /usr/sbin/service is a SHELL SCRIPT - the
       kernel therefore loads /usr/bin/dash and `audit.exe` is "/usr/bin/dash",
       never "/usr/sbin/service". The condition could not be satisfied. Same trap
       for any manager shipped as a script (ufw is a Python script, service a
       shell script).

       THIRD DEFECT, found while fixing the second: anchoring on `a0=` is not
       enough either. For a shebang script the kernel REWRITES the argv and
       inserts the interpreter first. Real trace of `service fail2ban stop`:
         a0="/bin/sh" a1="/usr/sbin/service" a2="fail2ban" a3="stop"
       The tool name sits in a1, not a0. So we anchor on `a\d+=`, i.e. any
       argument position, while still requiring the CO-OCCURRENCE of tool + verb
       + service to keep false positives down.
       WARNING: rules 100673/100674/100680/100681 still anchor on `a0=`. They
       only target real ELF binaries (zfs, rm, dd, wipefs, systemctl), so no
       false negative today - but adding a script-shipped tool there would
       reopen exactly this hole.

       Added: kill/pkill/killall (killing auditd without going through the
       service manager), update-rc.d / invoke-rc.d / chkconfig / initctl (other
       managers), aa-disable / aa-teardown / semanage (MAC), audispd.

       `audit.key = audit-wazuh-c`: restricted to execve events, so that file
       watches are not captured (same pattern as 100653).
       A bare `kill <pid>` stays undetectable (no service name in the argv) -
       that case is caught by the 100807/100801/100802 heartbeat, not by this
       rule.

       auditd purge: the three forms are covered explicitly. The former
       alternative `a\d+="(-D|0)"` missed the joined form `-e0` and matched any
       argument equal to "0" (FP).
       Possible FP: legitimate admin maintenance -> corroborate with context. -->

  <rule id="100654" level="12">
    <if_group>audit</if_group>
    <field name="audit.key" type="pcre2">^audit-wazuh-c$</field>
    <regex type="pcre2">a\d+="(?:[^"]*/)?(?i:systemctl|service|rc-service|invoke-rc\.d|update-rc\.d|chkconfig|initctl|kill|pkill|killall|setenforce|semanage|aa-disable|aa-teardown)"(?:[^\n]*?(?i:stop|disable|mask|-9|-15|-KILL|-TERM)[^\n]*?(?i:auditd|audispd|wazuh|rsyslog|syslog|journald|firewalld|falco|osquery|clamav|apparmor|fail2ban|snoopy)|[^\n]*?(?i:auditd|audispd|wazuh|rsyslog|syslog|journald|firewalld|falco|osquery|clamav|apparmor|fail2ban|snoopy)[^\n]*?(?i:stop|disable|mask|-9|-15|-KILL|-TERM))|a\d+="(?:[^"]*/)?(?i:auditctl)"[^\n]*?a\d+="-(?:D|e0)"|a\d+="(?:[^"]*/)?(?i:auditctl)"[^\n]*?a\d+="-e"\s+a\d+="0"|a\d+="(?:[^"]*/)?(?i:setenforce)"[^\n]*?a\d+="0"</regex>
    <description>Defense evasion: security/logging service tampered with ($(audit.command))</description>
    <mitre><id>T1562.001</id></mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
